<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Deliberately empty.

      Sundown needs no entitlements. Under the hardened runtime it can still
      read other same-user processes' arguments, send them signals, and spawn
      lsof and docker — none of those are entitlement-gated for processes you
      already own.

      What is NOT here, and why:

      · com.apple.security.app-sandbox
          Would break the entire product. A sandboxed process cannot signal
          processes outside its sandbox. This is also why the Mac App Store is
          closed to this app — see DISTRIBUTION.md.

      · com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
          Not needed, and asking for it weakens the hardened runtime for no
          gain.

      · com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      · com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
          No JIT, no dynamic codegen.

      · com.apple.security.automation.apple-events
          Sundown never scripts other apps.

      An empty entitlements file is not an oversight — it's the smallest
      possible claim on the user's machine, which is the point of the product.
      If a future change adds one, it needs a line here explaining why.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
